// Benchmark regression gate (#2931): builds and runs bench/cases/{collatz,
// mandelbrot,fib,alloc,churn,json,map,matrix,sort,strings} with the compiler
// under test and fails when peak RSS or user CPU time regresses past 1.5x a
// baseline recorded below, OR when allocated object count leaves a TWO-SIDED
// band (#3130, see "WHY OBJECT COUNT IS TWO-SIDED" below). "churn" (#2978) is
// a leak guard, not a speed benchmark: a live set of at most two objects
// across 4,000,000 allocations, run with BIT_GC_MIN_KB pinned via
// Baseline.gcMinKb -- see runtime/gc/gcindex.bit's idxGrow (#2756/#2922) for
// the bug this catches. Each of those objects is retained across the loop
// back edge and the last one is read at the print (#3667): before that the
// loop was dead code and #3622's dead-allocation elimination deleted it
// whole, taking the case to 7 objects with gcindex never entered.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. A 4-5x compute regression and an 18x peak-RSS regression
// both shipped across many releases with every gate in `./make test` staying
// green, because nothing in the suite measured performance at all — both were
// only found by a human running `bench/run.sh` by hand, 1470 commits after
// the fact. This is the gate that would have caught them the day they landed.
//
// WHY NOT `bench/run.sh`. That script needs `go` and `cc` on PATH, runs 7
// iterations per case per LANGUAGE (Bit, Go, C), and rewrites three tracked
// files (README.md, bench/RESULTS.md, bench/history.csv) — a gate must not
// mutate the tree, and 7x3 external-toolchain builds is not a suite cost.
// This measures Bit only, against baselines recorded in this file rather
// than read from bench/history.csv: its newest rows predate #2920 and still
// encode the bug (`alloc` RSS = 161529856), which is exactly the number
// #2920 fixed.
//
// WHY PEAK RSS AND USER CPU, NEVER WALL CLOCK. This repo already has three
// open tickets (#2401, #2415, #2422) about absolute wall-clock deadlines
// reddening the suite purely from host load. Peak RSS is a kernel-tracked
// high-water mark, unaffected by what else the box is doing. User CPU time is
// time actually spent executing on a core, not time spent waiting for one —
// measured on this host while writing this gate, `user` stayed within 0.06s
// across 7 runs of the same binary. `real` (wall clock) is never read below.
//
// WHY USER CPU IS NOT IMMUNE TO CONTENTION, ONLY LESS SENSITIVE (#3017). The
// paragraph above is correct but incomplete: "unaffected by what else the
// box is doing" is true of peak RSS, not of user CPU. During #2982's
// data-gathering session, at load average 30-44 with 2-3 concurrent
// make-driver processes, a clean, UNMUTATED fib binary measured 58cs
// against a 29cs baseline — exactly 2.0x — as a genuine PASSING reading,
// not a mutation (six consecutive runs that session: 54, 55, 40, 56, 58,
// 57cs). The same session recorded mandelbrot at 183/195/192/190cs against
// a 181cs ceiling and alloc at 347/493/483/539/473/521cs against a 346cs
// ceiling. Plausible mechanism, NOT confirmed: Apple Silicon P-core/E-core
// migration, where a low-QoS process pushed onto a slower E-core burns
// genuinely more CPU-seconds for the same instruction count, not merely
// longer wait time.
//
// This is not a live defect in the gate as deployed. test-benchgate is
// registered only as a Gate{} in tools/build/gates.bit, reachable only from
// `./make test`, which this repo runs once per push with the box to itself
// — never beside a concurrent build (see CLAUDE.md's verify loop). None of
// the ceilings in this file are calibrated to absorb load 30-44, and none
// should be: widening one on data from an environment this gate never runs
// in is how a check stops being a check (#2982's fib ceiling briefly did
// exactly that at 250% before being recalibrated against quiet-box data).
// This would matter only if this gate, or a future CPU-time-based one, is
// ever run deliberately alongside other builds.
//
// WHY MINIMUM OF N, NOT MEAN. Contention and scheduling noise can only ever
// make one run cost MORE than its true floor, never less, so the minimum of
// `reps` independent runs is the statistic noise cannot inflate — the same
// reasoning _tests_/bit/schedbench.bit's header gives for context-switch
// timing.
//
// WHY 1.5x, AND WHY IT NEVER FIRES ON AN IMPROVEMENT. The regressions this
// gate exists to catch were 4-5x and 18x — the bar only needs to clear gross
// breakage, not police day-to-day noise. The check is a strict
// greater-than against `baseline * 1.5`, so a benchmark that gets faster or
// leaner can only pass more easily, never fail. CPU (not RSS) can override
// this per case via Baseline.cpuCeilingPct — fib does (#2982), because its
// baseline is small enough in absolute terms that 1.5x cannot absorb real
// multi-agent scheduling noise; see fib's own baseline-table comment.
//
// WHY OBJECT COUNT IS TWO-SIDED (#3130), UNLIKE RSS/CPU ABOVE. The 1.5x bar
// above is deliberately one-directional because RSS and CPU are noisy: a
// benchmark that gets faster or leaner can only pass more easily, and that
// is correct, but it also means the gate protects yesterday's slow number
// forever — a later regression back to it still passes. Allocated object
// count (`swept+live` from `BIT_GC_STATS=1`, see measureScript's header
// below) has no such noise: it is EXACTLY deterministic, the same binary
// reporting the identical count on every run — verified 5x on every one of
// this file's 10 macOS/arm64 cases (#3130's ticket comment has all 15+
// readings). Determinism is what makes a TWO-SIDED band sound: failing past
// 1.05x (objCeiling) catches a regression the same way 1.5x does above, and
// failing below 0.5x (objFloor) catches an IMPROVEMENT that landed with no
// baseline update, which is the ratchet's whole point — a benchmark that
// gets leaner must have Baseline.objects re-recorded, not silently protect
// the old, higher number. Gate on objects, never on `collections`: the
// collection count depends on WHEN the trigger fires, which depends on live
// bytes, which #2941 currently inflates 8x — gating on it would produce
// exactly the flaky red that gets gates disabled. An absent or malformed
// `BIT_GC_STATS` line is a hard FAIL, never a pass (runCase's `m.objects < 0`
// check) — the line prints once, on the child's OWN stderr, only at a clean
// exit, so killing the process or discarding its stderr both produce
// nothing, and nothing must never parse as zero.
//
// WHY OUTPUTS ARE ASSERTED TOO. A benchmark that returns the wrong answer
// quickly is exactly the failure mode a pure-timing gate cannot see, so
// every run's stdout is compared byte-for-byte against the known-correct
// value before its cost is even considered.
//
// SUBPROCESS MECHANICS, as _tests_/bit/golden.bit and _tests_/bit/lspcache.bit:
// each case is built once with the compiler under test, then run `reps`
// times through a generated `/bin/sh` wrapper via `osRunBounded` (no rusage
// primitive is reachable from Bit itself). The wrapper is the only
// platform-specific part: macOS's `/usr/bin/time -l` reports both user CPU
// seconds and maximum resident set size (bytes) from ONE run; Linux has no
// such tool guaranteed present, so it backgrounds the binary and polls
// `/proc/<pid>/status` (`VmHWM`, itself a kernel-maintained high-water mark —
// any read taken late in the run already reflects the true peak) and
// `/proc/<pid>/stat` (utime+stime, the same two fields
// _tests_/bit/fsblock.bit's `cpu()` helper already reads and was mutation-
// tested for, #2207).
//
// Run from the repository root:
//   ./bit-out/bin/bit run _tests_/bit/benchgate
// Needs BIT_BIN (the compiler under test), BIT_LIBBITRT (the runtime
// archive to link against) and BIT_BENCH_DIR (bench/cases) — no defaults.
//
// #3023 split this file into `_tests_/bit/benchgate/{benchgate,baselines}.bit`:
// the per-platform baseline tables (`Baseline`, `platformName`,
// `baselinesMacosArm64`, `baselinesLinuxX64`, `baselinesLinuxArm64`,
// `baselinesFor`) now live in the sibling `baselines.bit`, no `import`, one
// shared scope. This file dropped from 809 to 534 lines and kept the ceiling
// math, the shell-wrapper plumbing, the per-case runner and `main`.
//
// #4508 added the STRING-FLIP ARM: every case with a reading in the new
// sibling `baselinesflip.bit` is built a SECOND time, run once, and its
// object count checked against that table for EXACT equality -- see
// `runFlipCase` and `checkFlipObjects` below, and that file's header. Owner
// ruling 2026-09-23 (#5755, #4411) put the flip ON by default, so this
// second build is now the one that exports `BIT_STRING_EXPLODE=0` (#3901's
// flip, read by `lowerProject` in compiler/lowerglobal.bit) — the DEFAULT
// build above is the flip arm now, and this second build is the reference
// off path. This arm reads only the deterministic metric: no CPU, no RSS,
// one run rather than `reps`.
//
// #4301 split this file again after it grew back to exactly 800 lines with
// zero headroom on the hard-zero E0200 gate: the SHELL PLUMBING, BUILD and
// MEASURE sections (`shQuote`, `mkdirQuiet`, `scratchRoot`, `runMsg`,
// `CompilerEnv`, `buildScript`, `darwinMeasureBranch`, `linuxMeasureBranch`,
// `measureScript`, `Measured`, `trimTrailingNl`, `parseMeasured`) now live
// in the sibling `benchrun.bit`, no `import`, same shared scope. This file
// dropped from 800 to 522 lines and kept the ceiling math, the tally, the
// per-case runner and `main`.
//
// #5095 made the CPU ceiling check contention-aware via an external host
// load probe (load average / core count). #5196 found that probe could
// never open on a well-populated box: it only tripped once load exceeded
// the TOTAL core count, so on an 18-core box it stayed "quiet" across the
// entire 1x-18x contention range this shared, multi-agent workspace
// actually produces, including the run that measured strmap at 165% of
// baseline under load 17.55 and nearly blocked a release. It was replaced
// with a same-run control: each rep's own `/usr/bin/time -l` (or, on Linux,
// wall-clock timestamps bracketing the same `/proc` poll — `benchrun.bit`'s
// darwinMeasureBranch()/linuxMeasureBranch()) already reports both wall-clock
// and total CPU time for that one measurement. `wasDescheduled()` below
// compares the two: a case whose user CPU exceeds its ceiling on a rep that
// was ALSO off a core for longer than it ran (gap > cpu, from the SAME
// reading) reports INCONCLUSIVE and does not fail the gate — no external
// probe, no load-average/core-count division, no calibrated threshold. RSS
// and object count are unaffected by contention (see WHY PEAK RSS above) and
// stay hard failures regardless, and an over-ceiling CPU reading on a rep
// that ran without interruption is still a hard FAIL — this narrows WHEN a
// regression is reported, never whether a real one can be.

import { env, envOr, exit } from "std/os"
import { readFile, exists, mkdir, writeFile } from "std/fs"
import { trimSpace, parseInt, split } from "std/strings"
import { runScript } from "../trampoline"

// Independent timed runs per case; the MINIMUM of each series is compared to
// baseline. 3 costs roughly one pass of the 4 cases' combined baseline CPU
// (~4.6s) times 3, plus 4 one-time builds — under 20s measured, inside the
// ~30s budget #2931 set (#2747's lint gate, at 21s, is the precedent for
// what a suite addition may cost).
const reps: int = 3

// FAIL when measured > baseline * 3 / 2 (i.e. > 1.5x). Integer arithmetic
// throughout so there is no float-rounding edge case in the comparison. Used
// for RSS on every case, and for CPU on every case except where
// Baseline.cpuCeilingPct overrides it -- see cpuCeiling() below and #2982.
fn ceiling(baseline: int): int {
  return baseline * 3 / 2
}

// CPU ceiling, per-case (#2982). `ceiling()` above is 150 (1.5x) for every
// case; fib overrides Baseline.cpuCeilingPct to 190 on macOS/arm64 ONLY,
// because its baseline there (29cs) is too small in absolute terms for 1.5x
// to absorb real scheduling noise in this gate's actual operating
// environment (a single unshared run, per the WHY USER CPU IS NOT IMMUNE TO
// CONTENTION note above). fib on Linux x64 keeps the blanket 150
// deliberately — its baseline there (101cs) gives 1.5x enough absolute
// headroom that the same defect does not apply; see fib's own
// baseline-table comment in each platform's table for the measurements.
// Integer arithmetic, same reasoning as ceiling() above.
fn cpuCeiling(b: Baseline): int {
  return b.cpuCentis * b.cpuCeilingPct / 100
}

// Object-count band (#3130), TWO-SIDED unlike ceiling()/cpuCeiling() above.
// Allocated object count (swept+live read from one BIT_GC_STATS=1 run, see
// gcObjects() below) is EXACTLY deterministic — the same binary reports the
// identical count on every run, verified 5x on every one of the 10
// macOS/arm64 cases (pasted in #3130's ticket comment) — so this guard can
// fail in BOTH directions. Past 1.05x (objCeiling) catches a regression the
// same way ceiling() does; below 0.5x (objFloor) catches an IMPROVEMENT that
// was never recorded as a new baseline, which is the reason #3130 exists: a
// benchmark that gets leaner must have Baseline.objects updated, not
// silently protect the old, slower number forever. Baseline.objects == -1
// means "not measured on this platform" (see its field comment in
// baselines.bit) — the band check is skipped for that case, but the GC
// stats line itself is still required unconditionally; see runCase's use of
// gcObjects() below for why a missing or malformed line is always a hard
// fail regardless of whether a numeric baseline is on file.
const objCeilPct: int = 105
const objFloorPct: int = 50

fn objCeiling(baseline: int): int {
  return baseline * objCeilPct / 100
}

fn objFloor(baseline: int): int {
  return baseline * objFloorPct / 100
}

// Generous per-subprocess bound; every case finishes in under 3s and this
// only matters if something hangs. `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S` widens it the same
// way every other harness in this suite honors it.
fn timeoutMs(): int {
  let s = parseInt(envOr("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S", "60")) catch _ {
    60
  }
  if (s <= 0) {
    return 60000
  }
  return s * 1000
}

// --- tally -----------------------------------------------------------------

class Tally { checks: int, failures: int, inconclusive: int }

fn markFail(t: Tally, msg: string) {
  t.failures = t.failures + 1
  eprint("FAIL benchgate: " + msg + "\n")
}

// #5095: an INCONCLUSIVE case is not counted as a failure — see this file's
// header — but it must never be silent, so it prints the same way a FAIL
// does and is tallied for the end-of-run summary in main().
fn markInconclusive(t: Tally, msg: string) {
  t.inconclusive = t.inconclusive + 1
  println("INCONCLUSIVE benchgate: " + msg)
}

fn minInt(a: int, b: int): int {
  if (a < b) {
    return a
  }
  return b
}

// --- one case ----------------------------------------------------------

// One case's build outcome. `binPath` is only meaningful when `ok` — see
// BuildResult{ok = false} sites below, which all leave it "".
class BuildResult { ok: bool, binPath: string }

// Derives this case's paths from `caseScratch`, then writes and runs its
// build script, marking `t` on any failure along the way. Extracted from
// runCase() (#3542) so the build phase carries its own complexity budget
// instead of adding to the driver's; paths are derived here (rather than
// passed in individually) to keep this at the same param count as
// buildScript()/runCase() rather than adding a new E0202 finding.
fn buildBenchCase(
  cc: CompilerEnv,
  benchDir: string,
  caseScratch: string,
  name: string,
  t: Tally,
): BuildResult {
  let srcPath = benchDir + "/" + name + "/" + name + ".bit"
  let binPath = caseScratch + "/bin"
  let buildOut = caseScratch + "/build.out"
  let buildErr = caseScratch + "/build.err"
  let buildSh = caseScratch + "/build.sh"

  let body = buildScript(cc, srcPath, binPath, buildOut, buildErr)
  let buildCode = runScript(buildSh, body, timeoutMs()) catch e {
    markFail(t, "${name}: cannot start the build script: " + e.message())
    return BuildResult{ ok = false, binPath = "" }
  }
  if (buildCode != 0) {
    let berr = readFile(buildErr) catch e {
      ""
    }
    markFail(t, "${name}: build failed (${runMsg(buildCode)}): ${berr}")
    return BuildResult{ ok = false, binPath = "" }
  }
  if (!exists(binPath)) {
    markFail(t, "${name}: build reported success but ${binPath} does not exist")
    return BuildResult{ ok = false, binPath = "" }
  }
  return BuildResult{ ok = true, binPath = binPath }
}

// One rep's launch outcome: the run script written and executed through
// `../trampoline`. `launched` is false only for a harness-side failure
// (write/spawn/timeout/crash — see runMsg()), never for a benchmark
// that ran and produced a bad result; that split is validateRep()'s job.
//
// #4702: the run script used to be chmod'd and handed to `osRunBounded`
// itself, so all 70 children one gate run starts (14 cases x reps, plus the
// builds and the flip leg) were the first exec of a brand-new file and paid
// macOS's system-wide provenance validation once each. Nothing about what is
// MEASURED moved: `measureScript`'s body is unchanged and still `exec`s the
// benchmark, so the process whose CPU and RSS are read is the same one.
class RepRun { launched: bool, msg: string, resText: string, outText: string }

// Extracted from runCase() (#3542): the filesystem/subprocess half of one
// repetition, with none of the result parsing.
fn runRepOnce(binPath: string, caseScratch: string, rep: int, gcMinKb: int): RepRun {
  let outPath = caseScratch + "/run${rep}.out"
  let resPath = caseScratch + "/run${rep}.res"
  let runSh = caseScratch + "/run${rep}.sh"
  // The wrapper's own exit re-raises the benchmark's exit code (see
  // measureScript's header), so a non-zero, non-timeout, non-signal code
  // here means the BENCHMARK itself failed, not the harness — validateRep()
  // below reads that case from the parsed result line instead.
  let body = measureScript(binPath, outPath, resPath, gcMinKb)
  let code = runScript(runSh, body, timeoutMs()) catch e {
    return RepRun{
      launched = false,
      msg = "cannot start run script: " + e.message(),
      resText = "",
      outText = "",
    }
  }
  if (code == 0 - 2 || code == 0 - 1 || code <= 0 - 100) {
    return RepRun{ launched = false, msg = runMsg(code), resText = "", outText = "" }
  }
  let resText = readFile(resPath) catch e {
    ""
  }
  let outText = readFile(outPath) catch e {
    ""
  }
  return RepRun{ launched = true, msg = "", resText = resText, outText = outText }
}

// One rep's measured outcome, parsed and checked against `b`. Never touches
// the filesystem or a subprocess — runRepOnce() above owns that half.
// Extracted from runCase() (#3542).
class RepCheck { ok: bool, msg: string, cpu: int, rss: int, obj: int, gap: int }

fn validateRep(resText: string, outText: string, b: Baseline): RepCheck {
  let m = parseMeasured(resText, outText)
  if (!m.ok) {
    return RepCheck{
      ok = false,
      msg = "no parseable result line (got '${resText}')",
      cpu = 0,
      rss = 0,
      obj = 0,
      gap = 0,
    }
  }
  if (m.exitCode != 0) {
    return RepCheck{
      ok = false, msg = "benchmark exited ${m.exitCode}", cpu = 0, rss = 0, obj = 0, gap = 0,
    }
  }
  // objects < 0 covers both a malformed result line AND a missing/absent
  // `BIT_GC_STATS` line (measureScript's awk prints -1 in either case, never
  // a silent 0) — this must be a hard fail unconditionally, even for a case
  // whose Baseline.objects is itself -1 ("not measured on this platform"): a
  // case with no recorded band still needs the underlying instrumentation
  // working, or a future re-baseline would read garbage.
  if (m.cpuCentis < 0 || m.rssBytes < 0 || m.objects < 0) {
    return RepCheck{
      ok = false,
      msg = "measurement unavailable (cpu=${m.cpuCentis} rss=${m.rssBytes} obj=${m.objects}) — " +
        "a -1 here can mean the BIT_GC_STATS line was missing or unparseable; never treat it as 0",
      cpu = 0, rss = 0, obj = 0, gap = 0,
    }
  }
  let got = trimSpace(trimTrailingNl(m.out))
  if (got != b.expected) {
    return RepCheck{
      ok = false,
      msg = "printed '${got}', want '${b.expected}' — wrong answer, cost not trusted",
      cpu = 0, rss = 0, obj = 0, gap = 0,
    }
  }
  return RepCheck{
    ok = true,
    msg = "",
    cpu = m.cpuCentis,
    rss = m.rssBytes,
    obj = m.objects,
    gap = m.gapCentis,
  }
}

// One case's minimum readings plus the descheduled-time gap (#5196) this
// gate saw on whichever rep produced `minCpu` — bundled so reportAndCheck()
// stays at 3 params instead of growing a 6th past E0202's 5-parameter limit;
// see runCase()'s reps loop for how `gapAtMin` is chosen.
class CaseMetrics { minCpu: int, minRss: int, minObj: int, gapAtMin: int }

// Prints the unconditional per-case summary line (#2931) and runs the
// RSS/object-count checks against `b`'s ceilings and floor, marking `t` for
// each one that trips; the CPU arm is checkCpu() below. Pure
// reporting/comparison — no filesystem, no subprocess. Extracted from
// runCase() (#3542).
// #5196: `gap` is the wall-clock time this rep's process spent off a core
// (real minus total CPU, from the SAME `/usr/bin/time -l` reading or Linux
// timestamp pair — see benchrun.bit's DESCHEDULE DETECTION note), `-1` when
// unavailable. Reuses `ceiling()` — the SAME 1.5x margin this file already
// trusts for the CPU/RSS regression bar above, not a second, independently
// guessed constant — against `cpu` instead of a baseline: `cpu + gap` is
// this rep's wall-clock time (real), so the check is real > 1.5x cpu, i.e.
// this rep spent at least as much wall time waiting for a core as it did
// running on one, beyond the modest headroom `ceiling()` already treats as
// noise everywhere else in this file. Self-relative and dimensionless (no
// load average, no core count) and it scales with the case's own reading
// rather than an absolute noise floor sized for one case — verified live on
// 2026-09-16 under real, unforced contention from a peer agent's #5322
// reproduction: mandelbrot and json breached ceiling with gap 0-1cs (their
// min-cpu rep ran uninterrupted) and correctly stayed FAIL; matrix and
// strmap breached ceiling with gap 44-45cs against cpu 53cs (real ~98/97cs,
// cpu*1.5 ~79/79cs) and correctly opened INCONCLUSIVE.
//
// IT CANNOT OPEN AT ALL FOR A MULTI-THREADED CASE, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL
// (#5732). `gap` is computed as `real - user - sys` and clamped at 0
// (benchrun.bit's darwinMeasureBranch/linuxMeasureBranch). The premise above
// — "`real` cannot be LESS than total CPU" — holds only while the process
// occupies at most one core. With N mutator threads total CPU runs up to N x
// real, so the subtraction is negative on every rep and the clamp reports 0:
// measured on bench/cases/allocpar (8 workers), gap was 0 on 45 of 45
// readings, quiet and loaded alike, while single-threaded `alloc` on the same
// box and the same instrument reported 0-46cs. So for a multi-mutator case
// this valve is welded shut and every over-ceiling CPU reading is a hard
// FAIL, contention or not — which is why allocpar's CPU is not gated at all
// rather than gated behind a valve that cannot open; see the MULTI-MUTATOR
// CPU note on `Baseline.cpuCentis` in baselinesdefs.bit.
fn wasDescheduled(gap: int, cpu: int): bool {
  return gap >= 0 && cpu + gap > ceiling(cpu)
}

// One case's CPU arm. Split out of reportAndCheck() (#5732) so the opt-out
// below is one guard in one place rather than a third condition on a
// function that already carries the deschedule branch.
//
// `Baseline.cpuCentis < 0` means this case's user CPU is NOT GATED — see
// that field's MULTI-MUTATOR CPU note in baselinesdefs.bit for the policy
// and the readings behind it. The reading is still taken and still printed
// by reportAndCheck() below; only the comparison is skipped, so the suite
// log still carries a real number for whoever re-baselines it.
fn checkCpu(b: Baseline, m: CaseMetrics, t: Tally) {
  if (b.cpuCentis < 0) {
    return
  }
  // CPU reads its own per-case ceiling (#2982); RSS always uses the blanket
  // 1.5x — see cpuCeiling()'s and Baseline.cpuCeilingPct's comments above.
  let cpuCeil = cpuCeiling(b)
  if (m.minCpu <= cpuCeil) {
    return
  }
  // #5196: contention can only ever inflate user CPU (see WHY USER CPU IS
  // NOT IMMUNE TO CONTENTION above), so an over-ceiling reading taken on a
  // rep the SAME measurement shows was descheduled for longer than it ran
  // cannot be told apart from a real regression — report INCONCLUSIVE,
  // naming the gap, and do not fail the gate for it. A reading whose own
  // rep ran without interruption is unchanged: FAIL.
  if (wasDescheduled(m.gapAtMin, m.minCpu)) {
    markInconclusive(
      t,
        "${b.name} user CPU regressed: ${m.minCpu}cs (min of ${reps}), " +
        "baseline ${b.cpuCentis}cs, ceiling ${cpuCeil}cs (${b.cpuCeilingPct}%), " +
        "descheduled ${m.gapAtMin}cs of wall time on that rep — " +
        "host contention or a genuine regression; re-run on a quiet host to tell them apart",
    )
    return
  }
  markFail(
    t,
      "${b.name} user CPU regressed: ${m.minCpu}cs (min of ${reps}), " +
      "baseline ${b.cpuCentis}cs, ceiling ${cpuCeil}cs (${b.cpuCeilingPct}%)",
  )
}

// The `cpu=` field of the per-case line below, which prints for every case
// whether or not checkCpu() above judges it (#5732): an ungated axis must
// say so in the log rather than print a ceiling nothing is compared to.
fn cpuReport(b: Baseline, minCpu: int): string {
  if (b.cpuCentis < 0) {
    return "cpu=${minCpu}cs (NOT GATED — see cpuCentis in baselinesdefs.bit)"
  }
  return "cpu=${minCpu}cs (baseline ${b.cpuCentis}cs, ceiling ${cpuCeiling(b)}cs)"
}

fn reportAndCheck(b: Baseline, m: CaseMetrics, t: Tally) {
  let rssCeil = ceiling(b.rssBytes)
  // objects is TWO-SIDED (#3130) — see objCeiling()/objFloor()'s comment
  // above. Baseline.objects == -1 means "not measured on this platform"; the
  // two checks below are then both skipped, but validateRep() already
  // required the stats line to be present regardless.
  let objCeil = objCeiling(b.objects)
  let objFlr = objFloor(b.objects)
  // Printed unconditionally, pass or fail, so a slow drift toward the
  // ceiling is visible in the suite log before it ever trips (#2931). The
  // gap reading is printed for every case, not only a failing one (#5196):
  // without a quiet-host distribution to compare against there is no way to
  // tell a working discriminator from a broken one.
  print(
      "benchgate: ${b.name} " +
      cpuReport(b, m.minCpu) +
      " " +
      "rss=${m.minRss}B (baseline ${b.rssBytes}B, ceiling ${rssCeil}B) " +
      "obj=${m.minObj} (baseline ${b.objects}, floor ${objFlr}, ceiling ${objCeil}) " +
      "descheduled ${m.gapAtMin}cs of wall time at min-cpu rep\n",
  )

  checkCpu(b, m, t)
  if (m.minRss > rssCeil) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} peak RSS regressed: ${m.minRss}B (min of ${reps}), " +
        "baseline ${b.rssBytes}B, ceiling ${rssCeil}B (150%)",
    )
  }
  if (b.objects >= 0 && m.minObj > objCeil) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} object count regressed: ${m.minObj} (min of ${reps}), " +
        "baseline ${b.objects}, ceiling ${objCeil} (${objCeilPct}%)",
    )
  }
  if (b.objects >= 0 && m.minObj < objFlr) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} object count fell below floor — an unrecorded improvement: " +
        "${m.minObj} (min of ${reps}), baseline ${b.objects}, floor ${objFlr} " +
        "(${objFloorPct}%) — re-measure and update Baseline.objects in " +
        "_tests_/bit/benchgate/baselines.bit if this is real",
    )
  }
}

// --- the string-flip arm (#4508) ---------------------------------------

// Reports and checks one case's flip-on object count against `want`
// (`flipObjectsFor`), then asserts the debt list on the MEASURED count.
//
// EXACT EQUALITY, not the [0.5x, 1.05x] band `reportAndCheck` applies to the
// shipped baselines, and this is the one place the two arms differ on
// purpose. Two reasons. (1) `swept+live` is exactly deterministic — the
// premise the WHY OBJECT COUNT IS TWO-SIDED note above already rests on, and
// re-verified for this table: 14 of 14 re-runs of the seven flip-on binaries
// reported identical counts. There is no noise for a band to absorb. (2) The
// band cannot do the job here. #4423 moved json's flip-on count -29.6%
// (32151094 -> 22626099) in one merge, and against the stale number that
// reading sits under the 1.05x ceiling AND over the 0.5x floor — a table
// describing a compiler two merges old would have passed. A percentage band
// tight enough to catch that (1%) still cannot fail on `map` or `matrix`,
// whose counts are 14 and 16: integer truncation puts 15 and 17 inside it.
// Equality has no such edge, needs no constants and no rounding rule, and
// every child of #3901 is EXPECTED to move these numbers — a re-measure is
// the point of the table, not an imposition on it.
fn checkFlipObjects(b: Baseline, got: int, want: int, t: Tally) {
  // Printed unconditionally, pass or fail, same as reportAndCheck's line.
  // `got`/`want` are this arm's flip-OFF reading and its recorded reference;
  // `b.objects` is the recorded flip-ON reference the default arm above is
  // checked against, band not equality.
  print(
      "benchgate: ${b.name} flip-off obj=${got} (flip-off baseline ${want}, " +
      "flip-on baseline ${b.objects})\n",
  )
  if (got != want) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} flip-off object count is ${got}, recorded ${want} — this count is " +
        "deterministic, so any difference is a real change: re-measure with " +
        "BIT_STRING_EXPLODE=0 exported to the compiler and update flipObjectsFor() " +
        "in _tests_/bit/benchgate/baselinesflip.bit, naming the commit",
    )
  }
  let debtMsg = flipDebtViolation(b.name, b.objects, got)
  if (debtMsg != "") {
    markFail(t, debtMsg)
  }
}

// Builds the case a SECOND time with #3901's string flip OFF
// (`BIT_STRING_EXPLODE=0`) and runs it once, checking its object count
// against `flipObjectsFor` — the recorded flip-off reference, now that the
// default build above is the flip-on arm. Skipped entirely — no build, no
// run — for any case with no flip reading on file, which is every case on
// every platform but macOS/arm64.
//
// One run, not `reps`: the only metric read here is `swept+live`, which is
// exactly deterministic (the WHY OBJECT COUNT IS TWO-SIDED note above, and
// re-verified for this table -- see checkFlipObjects), so repetitions buy
// nothing. CPU and RSS are deliberately NOT checked on this
// arm — flip-off is not a shipped configuration, and those two are the noisy
// metrics this file's header warns about. `validateRep` still compares the
// program's stdout against `b.expected`, so a flip that changes the ANSWER
// fails here rather than being scored on allocation alone.
//
// The flip build gets its own scratch subdirectory because buildBenchCase()
// derives `binPath` from the directory it is handed; sharing one would have
// the second build overwrite the binary the flip-on arm just measured.
fn runFlipCase(cc: CompilerEnv, benchDir: string, caseScratch: string, b: Baseline, t: Tally) {
  let want = flipObjectsFor(b.name, hostTarget())
  if (want < 0) {
    return
  }
  let flipScratch = caseScratch + "/flip"
  mkdirQuiet(flipScratch)
  let ccFlip = CompilerEnv{
    bit = cc.bit,
    libbitrt = cc.libbitrt,
    stdlib = cc.stdlib,
    explodeStrings = false,
  }
  let built = buildBenchCase(ccFlip, benchDir, flipScratch, b.name, t)
  if (!built.ok) {
    return
  }
  let rr = runRepOnce(built.binPath, flipScratch, 0, b.gcMinKb)
  if (!rr.launched) {
    markFail(t, "${b.name} flip: ${rr.msg}")
    return
  }
  let rc = validateRep(rr.resText, rr.outText, b)
  if (!rc.ok) {
    markFail(t, "${b.name} flip: ${rc.msg}")
    return
  }
  checkFlipObjects(b, rc.obj, want, t)
}

// Builds the case once, runs it `reps` times, and checks the minimum against
// `b`'s baselines. Split for complexity (#3542) into buildBenchCase() (the
// build), runRepOnce()+validateRep() (one repetition's launch and its
// result), and reportAndCheck() (the ceiling/floor comparisons) — this
// function is now only the sequencing between them.
fn runCase(cc: CompilerEnv, benchDir: string, scratch: string, b: Baseline, t: Tally) {
  let caseScratch = scratch + "/" + b.name
  mkdirQuiet(caseScratch)

  let built = buildBenchCase(cc, benchDir, caseScratch, b.name, t)
  if (!built.ok) {
    return
  }
  let binPath = built.binPath

  // Sentinels above any measurement this gate could ever legitimately see —
  // the check after the loop proves a real run replaced them, the same shape
  // _tests_/bit/schedbench.bit's `benchTrials` uses.
  let minCpu = 1000000000
  let minRss = 1000000000
  let minObj = 1000000000
  // #5196: the descheduled-time gap paired with whichever rep's CPU became
  // minCpu — RSS and object count are unaffected by contention (see
  // reportAndCheck) so they need no reading of their own. -1 ("not
  // measured") is unreachable once sawOne is true, since every successful
  // rep updates gapAtMin below from that SAME rep's own measurement.
  let gapAtMin = -1
  let sawOne = false

  let rep = 0
  while (rep < reps) {
    let rr = runRepOnce(binPath, caseScratch, rep, b.gcMinKb)
    if (!rr.launched) {
      markFail(t, "${b.name} rep ${rep}: ${rr.msg}")
      rep = rep + 1
      continue
    }
    let rc = validateRep(rr.resText, rr.outText, b)
    if (!rc.ok) {
      markFail(t, "${b.name} rep ${rep}: ${rc.msg}")
      rep = rep + 1
      continue
    }
    if (rc.cpu < minCpu) {
      minCpu = rc.cpu
      gapAtMin = rc.gap
    }
    minRss = minInt(minRss, rc.rss)
    minObj = minInt(minObj, rc.obj)
    sawOne = true
    rep = rep + 1
  }

  if (!sawOne) {
    markFail(t, "${b.name}: no successful run out of ${reps}")
    return
  }

  reportAndCheck(
    b,
    CaseMetrics{ minCpu = minCpu, minRss = minRss, minObj = minObj, gapAtMin = gapAtMin },
    t,
  )
  // #4508's second, flip-on build. After reportAndCheck so a case that is
  // already failing its shipped baselines says so first, and outside the
  // `t.checks` count deliberately — main() compares that against the table
  // length, and the flip arm is per-case optional, not a case of its own.
  runFlipCase(cc, benchDir, caseScratch, b, t)
  t.checks = t.checks + 1
}

// --- driver ------------------------------------------------------------

// Fails the whole gate immediately (never returns) when `value` — expected
// to be an existing path read from `label`'s env var — is empty or does not
// exist. Extracted from main() (#3542): the three BIT_BIN/BIT_LIBBITRT/
// BIT_BENCH_DIR checks each cost main() its own branch inline.
fn requireEnvPath(label: string, value: string) {
  if (value == "" || !exists(value)) {
    eprint("FAIL benchgate: ${label}='${value}' does not exist — refusing to guess\n")
    exit(1)
  }
}

fn main() {
  let bit = envOr("BIT_BIN", "")
  requireEnvPath("BIT_BIN", bit)
  let libbitrt = envOr("BIT_LIBBITRT", "")
  requireEnvPath("BIT_LIBBITRT", libbitrt)
  let benchDir = envOr("BIT_BENCH_DIR", "")
  requireEnvPath("BIT_BENCH_DIR", benchDir)
  let stdlib = env("BIT_STDLIB")

  let target = hostTarget()
  let table = baselinesFor(target)
  if (len(table) == 0) {
    eprint(
        "FAIL benchgate: no baseline recorded for ${platformName(target)}; " +
        "measure one on a quiet host and add it to _tests_/bit/benchgate/baselines.bit\n",
    )
    exit(1)
  }

  let root = scratchRoot()
  mkdirQuiet(root)

  // explodeStrings: true — the shipped configuration since the owner ruling
  // 2026-09-23 (#5755, #4411). runFlipCase() builds its own CompilerEnv with
  // the flip off; nothing turns it off for this one.
  let cc = CompilerEnv{ bit = bit, libbitrt = libbitrt, stdlib = stdlib, explodeStrings = true }
  let t = Tally{ checks = 0, failures = 0, inconclusive = 0 }
  for b of table {
    runCase(cc, benchDir, root, b, t)
  }

  // Compared against the actual table length, not a hardcoded count (#2978):
  // platforms register different numbers of cases (baselinesLinuxX64 lacks
  // "churn"), so a fixed constant here would under-count a genuine failure
  // on whichever platform has the larger table.
  if (t.checks < len(table)) {
    eprint("FAIL benchgate: only ${t.checks} of ${len(table)} cases completed\n")
    t.failures = t.failures + 1
  }
  // #5095: printed regardless of pass/fail, same reason stress's
  // reportJudged() always prints its inconclusive count — an unjudged case
  // must never look like a silent pass, and does not affect the exit code.
  if (t.inconclusive > 0) {
    println(
        "benchgate: ${t.inconclusive} case(s) INCONCLUSIVE under host contention " +
        "— re-run them on a quiet host to tell contention from a real regression",
    )
  }
  if (t.failures > 0) {
    println("benchgate: FAIL — ${t.failures} problem(s) across ${t.checks} case(s)")
    teardown(root, timeoutMs())
    exit(1)
  }
  teardown(root, timeoutMs())
  println("benchgate: PASS — ${t.checks} case(s) within their RSS/CPU/object baselines")
}
